The stroke-width value is conditioned on `look === 'neo'`, but the CSS rule that uses it (`section-${i-1} rect, path, circle, polygon { stroke-width: ... }`) is NOT scoped with a `[data-look="neo"]` selector. This means when `look === 'neo'`, the narrower stroke width is written into the unscoped `.section-X` CSS and applied to all nodes regardless of their `data-look` attribute value. All other neo-specific visual changes in this PR correctly use `[data-look="neo"]` scoping. The stroke-width rule should follow the same pattern:
```css
[data-look="neo"].mindmap-node.section-${i-1} rect, ... {
  stroke-width: ${Math.max(10 - (i - 1) * 2, 2)};
}
```
And the classic stroke-width rule should remain as the unscoped baseline.
